ALGORITHM: SELECTION-SORT(A, n)

1. For i = 0 to n-2

2.      min = i

3.      For j = i+1 to n-1

4.          If A[j] < A[min]
                min = j

5.      Exchange A[i] and A[min]

6. End For


TIME COMPLEXITY
Number of comparisons:

(n-1) + (n-2) + ... + 2 + 1

= n(n-1)/2

Therefore,

Best Case    = Θ(n^2)
Average Case = Θ(n^2)
Worst Case   = Θ(n^2)





WHY BEST CASE IS NOT BETTER -
Even if the array is already sorted,
Selection Sort still searches for the
minimum element in every remaining part.

Therefore, best-case time is also Θ(n^2).
